<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thutmose III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1479–1425 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thutmose III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> and one of the most successful military rulers in its history. After the co-regency with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, he conducted numerous campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, including the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a>, and consolidated Egyptian control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Syria-Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Accounts of the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> treat him as a central figure in the empire's foundation, economy and military.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii.jpg" alt="Thutmose III" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ولاء · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_Museum,_Egypt_49_(trim).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Late Bronze Age, after <b>Thutmose III</b>’s campaigns brought the Levantine coast under Egyptian control, Byblos served as a loyal base for the pharaohs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hatshepsut, who ruled as pharaoh alongside the young <b>Thutmose III</b> from approximately 1479 to 1458 BCE, consolidated this inheritance through trade rather than war, dispatching the celebrated expedition to Punt and sponsoring an extraordinary building programme at Karnak and Deir el-Bahari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mitanni soon became Egypt's chief rival in Syria, the target of <b>Thutmose III</b>'s campaign across the Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…speak of the Keftiu, usually taken to be the Cretans, and paintings in the Theban tombs of officials of Hatshepsut and <b>Thutmose III</b> in the 15th century BCE, among them Senenmut and Rekhmire, show men with long locks of hair and patterned kilts bringing Aegean vessels as gifts.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>They were trained as scribes, wore fine white linen, and were rewarded by the king: <b>Thutmose III</b> gave his official Minmose more than 150 captive “dependents”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharaoh <b>Thutmose III</b> routed a coalition led by the king of Kadesh at Megiddo, on a date traditionally reckoned to 16 April.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
